{
"dur": 44.365,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 6.87,
"text": "Then every NATO convoy hits the same bottleneck: the Suwa\u0142ki Gap, a strip of land along the Polish-Lithuanian border.",
"end": 7.145
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 7.145,
"vo_start": 7.42,
"vo_end": 17.139,
"text": "It is only about 65 kilometres wide. On one side is Kaliningrad, a heavily armed piece of Russia. On the other is Belarus, Russia's closest ally.",
"end": 17.414
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 17.414,
"vo_start": 17.689,
"vo_end": 26.547,
"text": "Missiles, drones and artillery from both sides could turn the roads into a gauntlet. If the gap closes, the Baltics can only be reached by sea or air.",
"end": 26.822
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 26.822,
"vo_start": 27.097,
"vo_end": 34.095,
"text": "That makes the Baltic Sea vital, and one island most of all: Gotland, owned by Sweden, a NATO member since 2024.",
"end": 34.37
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 34.37,
"vo_start": 34.645,
"vo_end": 43.415,
"text": "Experts call it an unsinkable aircraft carrier. Jets from Gotland could hit Russian supply lines, and Sweden says it could be ready for action within days.",
"end": 44.365
}
],
